<p>
I want to practice French pronunciation while I'm walking around or biking in a relatively safe place. Having done the work to extract the tongue twisters and expressions from my recordings, it was pretty easy to use <a href="https://sachachua.com/dotemacs#writing-and-editing-learning-french-extract-tonguetwisters">some Emacs Lisp</a>, <a href="https://github.com/sachac/subed-record">subed-record</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/sachac/compile-media">compile-media</a> to create individual audio files that I could play in shuffle mode. Each file has ten repetitions of a chime, the tongue twister, and enough silence so that I can repeat the phrase. The chime makes it possible for me to say the phrase in unison with the sample, or I can wait until the silent part in order to see how much I remember.
</p>

<p>
To make that, I use <a href="https://github.com/sachac/subed-record/blob/main/subed-record.el">subed-record-extract-last-phrases</a> to create a VTT file, edit it
to adjust the timestamps (<a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2026/09/practising-french-pronunciation-on-the-go-experimenting-with-a-bluetooth-phone-controller-ring/2026-09-22-virelangues-script.vtt">sample file</a>), use <a href="https://github.com/sachac/subed-record/blob/main/subed-record.el">subed-record-compile-video</a> to create the clip compilation (<a href="https://www.youtube.com/shorts/loibtm8mOm8">example video</a>), and then use <a href="https://sachachua.com/dotemacs#writing-and-editing-learning-french-extract-tonguetwisters">sacha-learn-lang-make-tonguetwister-practice-files</a> to create the clips. Here's an example of one tonguetwister repeated 10 times:
</p>

